---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const size = assertion('two-thousand-two-hundred-and-twenty-five-miles')
const owner = assertion('one-mile-in-six-has-an-owner')
const i75 = assertion('a-hundredth-of-the-road-and-a-third-of-the-traffic')
const change = assertion('every-ownership-change-is-a-corporation-line')
const lima = assertion('a-thirtieth-of-the-ground-and-an-eighth-of-the-road')
const head = assertion('three-times-the-road-per-head-outside-lima')
const drawn = assertion('one-interstate-drawn-twice')
const frozen = assertion('the-map-stopped-before-the-county-did')

const spec = (a: typeof size, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const ownerSpec = spec(owner, 'Centerline miles')
const headSpec = spec(head, 'Miles of road per 1,000 residents')
const frozenSpec = spec(frozen, 'Driveable centerline miles')
---

<Article slug="one-mile-in-six">
  <p>
    This corpus knew Allen County's bridges by owner and condition back to 1992, its road deaths
    back to 1975 and its railroads back to 1883. It had never counted a mile of highway. Two files
    close that, and they disagree about the county by a factor of two before they agree about
    anything.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={size} open />
  <p>
    That is 5.53 miles for every square mile of ground and one bridge for every 6.11 miles of
    pavement. It is also the last uncomplicated sentence in this piece.
  </p>

  <h2>Who owns it</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={owner} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="owner"
    title="The sixth that has an owner"
    caption="Federal-aid highway miles in Allen County by owner, 2018. The four together are 359.2 miles of a 2,225.6-mile network; the state's half of them carries 72.0 per cent of the traffic."
    columns={['Owner', 'Miles']}
    rows={owner.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="owner" set:html={JSON.stringify(ownerSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={i75} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={change} />
  <p>
    Which says something about how this county is actually divided. The state and the county and the
    townships hold their roads whole; the only place a road changes hands is where it crosses into a
    village or a city. A corporation line is the one boundary that reaches down to the pavement.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it is</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={lima} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={head} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="head"
    title="Road per head"
    caption="Miles of road per thousand residents, 2024 linework against 2024 population. The figure that a city resident's density inverts."
    columns={['Where', 'Miles per 1,000 people']}
    rows={head.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(headSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Both readings are true and they point opposite ways. Lima has the road packed twenty miles to
    the square mile and the townships have it spread thin; per person, the arithmetic reverses and
    the thin version is the expensive one. Nothing in either file carries a surface or a width, so
    the corpus stops there.
  </p>

  <h2>What a mile is</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={drawn} />
  <Gloss>
    A category in a file is obviously somebody's word. A polyline is obviously the shape of the
    thing — and it is not. Interstate 75 is divided, so the Census Bureau draws it twice and the
    Federal Highway Administration measures it once, and both are correct about different
    quantities. The tell is the ratio: two independent measurements of one road differ by a few per
    cent, not by 2.0030. The same defect runs the other way at the seams, where a road on a township
    line lies inside both townships and this county's thirteen subdivisions sum to 2,314.1 miles
    against a county total of 2,225.6.
  </Gloss>
  <AssertionCard assertion={frozen} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="frozen"
    title="Six editions of one map"
    caption="Driveable centerline miles in the Census Bureau's road file for Allen County. The last three editions are identical to a tenth of a mile."
    columns={['Edition', 'Miles']}
    rows={frozen.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="frozen" set:html={JSON.stringify(frozenSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    So the series cannot be differenced in either direction. The flat part is the Bureau not
    revisiting the county, and the moves before it are the Bureau revisiting it: US 30 kept its
    48.098 miles across the edition that reclassified 3.689 of them, and the county's secondary
    total fell by almost exactly that. Nothing was built. A label moved on existing pavement.
  </p>
</Article>
